Yes. This is a strong Phase 1 direction, but I would make one important correction before calling it the “perfect version.”


The biggest architectural principle should be:




WORLD // NEXUS must never confuse cryptographic integrity with truth.


A Merkle proof can prove that NEXUS recorded an assertion. A zk-proof can prove that a computation followed specified rules. Neither, by itself, proves that the underlying real-world claim is true.




With that distinction, I would evolve your Phase 1 into a production architecture like this:


WORLD // NEXUS — Phase 1.1 Core


1. World-State Fabric




Globally distributed bitemporal event/state ledger


Immutable append-only assertions


Current-state materialized views


Historical reconstruction at arbitrary valid_time + transaction_time


H3-based spatial partitioning


Entity/relationship graph


Explicit uncertainty and provenance




2. Evidence & Provenance Layer
Every assertion becomes something like:


ASSERTION → SOURCE → OBSERVATION → TRANSFORMATION → DERIVATION → CONFIDENCE


And every transformation receives a deterministic provenance record.


Cryptographic primitives:




content hashes


Merkle/DAG commitments


signed source attestations


reproducible transformation manifests


optional zk proofs


periodic external anchoring




This gives you verifiability without pretending verification equals truth.


3. Consensus Layer


I would not make H3 cells themselves the fundamental Raft consensus boundary.


Instead:


Global Control Plane
↓

Regional Shards

↓

H3 Spatial Partitions

↓

Entity/Temporal Partitions


That prevents geography from becoming an unnecessary constraint on entities that move, overlap multiple cells, or have global relationships.


